Background. A complex algebraic variety $X$ is said to have symplectic singularities (in the sense of Beauville) if it is normal, its smooth locus carries a holomorphic symplectic form $\omega$, and for any resolution of singularities $\pi: \tilde{X} \to X$ the pullback $\pi^*\omega$ extends to a regular (possibly degenerate) 2-form on $\tilde{X}$. Such a variety admits a finite stratification into symplectic leaves, and the closure relations among these leaves form a finite poset called the Hasse diagram of $X$.

For two leaves $L' \subset \overline{L}$ that are adjacent in the Hasse diagram (i.e., no leaf lies strictly between them in the closure order), the transverse slice to $L'$ inside $\overline{L}$ at a point of $L'$ is a (germ of a) conical symplectic singularity whose Hasse diagram has exactly two elements. Such a transverse slice is called an elementary slice.

Known examples of elementary slices include:
(i) Kleinian (du Val) surface singularities $\mathbb{C}^2/\Gamma$ for finite $\Gamma \subset \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{C})$, classified by simply-laced Dynkin diagrams;
(ii) closures of minimal nilpotent orbits in complex simple Lie algebras;
(iii) certain slices between adjacent nilpotent orbit closures in classical and exceptional Lie algebras;
(iv) elementary slices arising as transverse slices in affine Grassmannian slices and in Coulomb branches of 3d $\mathcal{N}=4$ quiver gauge theories.

Problem. Give a complete classification of all elementary slices, i.e., of all conical symplectic singularities whose Hasse diagram has exactly two elements, including those that do not arise from affine Grassmannian slices or from any of the known constructions above.

